            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 12 — How Nature Works in Harmony"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.886,
            0.029,
            0.923,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "201"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.746,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "important nutrients to the soil. You can also find tiny insects, such as beetles and flies, on animal droppings—like elephant dung—as they help break it down and recycle nutrients, back into the environment. This process is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "decomposition",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and the organisms carrying out the process are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "decomposers",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "saprotrophs",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (sapro= rotten + trophs = food). Plants grow in soil and many of the nutrients in soil come from the decomposition process. Thus, decomposers play an important role in recycling nutrients. In nature, nothing is wasted—everything is reused. Does nature really waste anything?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.282,
            0.346,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ever heard of ..."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.32,
            0.744,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "India is a country with diverse habitats and seasons. Many "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "migratory birds",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " fly thousands of miles and reach different habitats in India. They migrate from different parts of the world to avoid harsh climate, and in search for food. Birds not only enhance the aesthetics of those habitats but also play a significant role in keeping the balance in an ecosystem as "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "pollinators",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or seed dispersers along the path they migrate. This way they link two habitats. These birds are "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "predators",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of insect pests and help farmers to control pest populations, and indirectly help in healthy crop growth. Migratory birds, Demoiselle Crane visit the water body of Khichan village in Jodhpur district during the winter months. Do you know which birds in your area are seen only during winter? "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Collect",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " postal stamps and covers of migratory birds released by the Indian Postal Department, and collect information about their place of origin and reasons for their migration to different localities in India and more. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Showcase",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the postal stamps in your science laboratory/school library to popularise migratory birds."
            }
          ]
        },
